Abstract
The Basil seeds mucilaginous polysaccharide exhibits remarkable physical and chemical properties like high water-absorbing capacity, emulsifying, and stabilizing properties. The metal-organic frameworks are one of the most promising precursors made of metal clusters and organic connectors for the fabrication of advanced adsorbents due to their unique properties. In this study, the bionanocomposite of magnetic zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 was successfully synthesized and applied to adsorb azo cationic and anionic dyes. The synthesized magnetite nanoparticles were pretreated with mucilage extracted from basil seeds to acquire negatively charged magnetite surface, followed by nucleation through attracting zinc cation, and then the growth of metal-organic frameworks which yields high-quality ZIF-8 crystals. The samples were characterized by Field Scanning Electron Microscopy, X-ray Diffraction, Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometry, vibrating sample magnetometer, and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area analysis. In the process of adsorption, influencing
factors and recycling regeneration were discussed, and the adsorption mechanisms such as kinetics, isotherm, and thermodynamics were explored. The results of the adsorption process showed that maximum adsorption capacities were 9.09 and \(13.21~\mathrm{mg/g}\) for Methylene blue and Eriochrome Black T, respectively. The excellent reusability combined with its magnetic separation property makes the nanocomposite a promising adsorbent for the removal of cationic and anionic dyes.
Keywords: Bionanocomposite; Dye removal; Zeolitic imidazolate framework
## 1. Introduction
As a natural resource, water is one of the most urgent needs of life. However, with the intensification of human activities and the fast progress of modern industries, water pollution has become a serious problem all over the world [1-3]. Water pollution is also a critical factor that can alter the supply-demand relationship in water resources [4, 5]. Therefore, wastewater treatment is the key to achieving the purity needed for various uses, including drinking water[6, 7]. Common wastewater pollutants mainly include heavy metal ions, radioactive substances, polyaromatic hydrocarbons, pesticides, drugs, and dyes [8-14]. These pollutants can be divided into organic and inorganic categories according to their chemical composition. Most organic pollutants pose a serious threat to the environment and human health because of their impermeability, toxicity, and carcinogenicity [15, 16] and are much wider and more complex than heavy metal pollution. Environmental organic pollutants are mainly created by two sources natural resources and human resources. Antibiotics, dyes, oils, pesticides, and persistent organic contaminants are among the types of organic pollutants [17, 18]. Among the organic pollutants stated, dyes are usually organic compounds with complex structures and high toxicity [19-21]. Dyes in water pose significant risks to human health. Since many of them are toxic, they can cause skin irritation and allergy and are mutagenic and
carcinogenic [22]. Methylene blue (MB) is a cationic dye [23] that is not very toxic to humans and animals. However, it can increase heart rate, nausea, and vomiting during inhalation, and may stimulate eye and skin and systemic effects of accidental exposure, including cyanosis [24-26]. Moreover, MB has a negative effect on the environment and damages the ecosystem balance. This dye prevents the penetration of light for aquatic organisms and thus threatens life. Hence, the removal of this dye from water is an important issue for environmental protection [27]. Eriochrome black T (EBT) represents carcinogenic azo anionic color compounds [28]. EBT is commonly used for dyeing wool, silk and nylon fibers. EBT in sewage and groundwater has a major effect on human health due to its toxicity and carcinogenicity, so it must be excreted before entering the body [29]. With the intensification of colored sewage pollution, the treatment of colored sewage, and the prevention of secondary pollution, it is of economic and practical importance to reduce the crisis of water resources. A variety of techniques including ion exchange, supercritical fluid extraction, filtration, electrodialysis, deposition, microbial system, electrochemical process, advanced oxidation process, and membrane bioreactors, coagulation-flocculation, chemical deposition, buoyancy and reverse osmosis dyes and other pollutants are used by the sewage with their advantages and disadvantages. Process efficiency, environmental compatibility, and economic feasibility restrict the use of the conventional methods described above. Adsorption is the simplest and most economical method for removing paints and other pollutants from wastewater due to its effectiveness [22, 30-34]. Process adsorption is rich in scientific and experimental background and is extremely flexible in operation and design. In the adsorption process, the adsorbents can be resuscitated using simple heat transfer mechanisms or possibly chemical washing, resuscitation and reabsorption. Revitalization and reusability make the process of surface adsorption economically viable and justifiable [35-38]. Among the adsorbent, activated carbon, polymer resins, zeolites or alumina silicates, and
agricultural wastes can be cited [39]. On the other hand, different composites prepared from nano and bio-materials such as chitosan/\(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\)-hydroxyapatite nanocomposite beads [40], cellulose/clay bionanocomposite [41], nano-zerovalent manganese/biochar composite [42], chitosan/Ag-hydroxyapatite nanocomposite beads [43], etc. are developed for enhanced dye removal from aqueous solutions. However, some inherent problems like low specific surface area and incompatibility have negative effects on the performance of some of these adsorbents. In this regard, the size and structure of the adjustable pores, the large number of active sites, and the easy charge separation with stability in the resonance structure under ambient light make the metal-organic frameworks a good candidate for adsorbents or photocatalysts [1]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of high-porosity crystalline materials that have been developed with extremely high surface area [44], formed by strong bonds between metal ions and organic binders [45]. Porous materials like porous ceramics, zeolites, activated charcoal, porous metal, polymeric foam, and porous glass are used in many ways in our daily lives. Given its famous properties and various uses, the field of porous materials, especially nano-porous materials, has been rapidly developing over the past two decades. Unlike other porous materials, MOFs are a branch of material that has many properties like thermal stability [46], discrete regular structure, ultra-low density, large internal surface area, ease of synthesis and a wide range of properties suitable for physical and chemical uses. These great features have caused endless research into the development of many new porous materials [47]. The zeolitic imidazolate frameworks are a subset of metal and organic frameworks that have recently received much attention and attention. Given its exceptional chemical and thermal properties and its high specificity and high performance, it is an efficient and recyclable adsorbent [48, 49]. Microporous materials such as zeolitic imidazolate frameworks are suitable for catalysis and separation applications. These high surface area materials are invariably produced in particulate forms and need to be
transformed into hierarchically porous structures for high performance adsorbents or catalysts. Structuring of porous powders enables an optimized structure with high mass transfer, low pressure drop, good heat management, and high mechanical and chemical stability. According to the above explanation, the key novelty of the present work is that the Zn-based magnetic zeolitic imidazolate framework bionanocomposite could serve as adsorbent against azo dyes from water for treatment applications. In this study, the magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles with good performance were successfully prepared by the precipitation method. Next, the magnetic nanoparticles were coated with basalt seed mucilage and then combined with ZIF-8 to obtain the final nanocomposite. Finally, the synthesized nanocomposite was exposed to aqueous MB and EBT solutions under various conditions to examine the effect of pH parameters on dye removal, kinetics, isotherm and thermodynamics of the adsorption and regeneration process of the adsorbent.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
The chemicals used in this study were of laboratory-type without any further purification: 2-methyl imidazole, zinc nitrate \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), sodium hydroxide, hydrochloric acid, MB (Merck), 96% methanol, deionized water, iron 3 chloride \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), iron 2 chloride 3 chloride \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Chem-Lab), basil seed, EBT (Uni-Chem) were prepared.
## 2.2 Preparation of zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8)
First, \(4.83\mathrm{~g~}\) of 2-methyl imidazole was added to \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) of methanol and then to \(0.5\mathrm{~g~}\) of nitrate \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(15\,\mathrm{mL}\) of methanol to synthesize ZIF-8, and each solution was stirred separately for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) by a magnetic stirrer. The solution containing zinc nitrate \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added to the solution containing 2-methyl imidazole and stirred for 20 hours with a magnetic
stirrer. After 20 hours, the resulting milky solution was centrifuged three times each for 20 min and was washed with methanol or water. Finally, the remaining matter was dried in an oven for 24 hours at \(60^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\).
## 2.3 Basil Seed Mucilage Extract (MC)
First, \(42{\mathrm{~g~}}\) of basil seed was added to \(1400~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water. As soon as the basil seed is exposed to water, a gelatinous material is formed around the seed, which is used to distribute the nanoparticles uniformly. The mixture was stirred for 5 minutes with a magnetic stirrer and the impurities were floated on the water surface for 5 min. To increase the extraction efficiency, the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the mixture was adjusted to about 9.9 with \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) sodium hydroxide. The mixture was stirred by a high-speed magnetic stirrer for 24 hours again. After 24 hours, the mucilage formed from the mixture was removed by filtration and dried in an oven at \(50^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) for 10 hours.
## 2.4 Synthesis of magnetic nanoparticles (NP)
In this study, magnetic iron nanoparticles were synthesized by the co-precipitation method using precursors of iron II, chloride \(4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and iron III, chloride \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with a molar ratio of 1: 2. First, \(4.293\mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(11.674\mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were added to \(30~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water and stirred for 5 min with a magnetic stirrer. Then, the solution was discarded from the filter paper and the solution was subjected to magnetic stirring in a three-way balloon to remove the impurities. The first inlet was used for nitrogen gas input, the second inlet for 1M NaOH, and the third inlet for oxygen flow outlet. First, for 5 minutes, only the nitrogen flow entered and the oxygen flows out, and then the 1 M NaOH drop was added for 1 hour. After finishing the addition of the solution, the solution was exposed to nitrogen gas for 30 minutes. During the process, the solution color changed from yellow to light brown and then
to dark brown and finally to black. The black solution was then incubated in the oven for 9 hours at \(40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Finally, the black precipitate formed by a strong magnet was washed 5 times with distilled water and dried in an oven at 80 for 24 hours.
## 2.5 Synthesis of Magnetite Nanoparticles Modified with Basil Seed Mucilage (NP/MC)
In this study, \(4\,\mathrm{g}\) of magnetic iron nanoparticles were first added to \(1000\,\mathrm{mL}\) ionized water and then under ultrasonic bath for 1 hour to synthesize nanoparticles coated with basil seed mucilage. After 1 hour, \(2\,\mathrm{g}\) of basil seed mucilage was added to the solution and again subjected to an ultrasonic bath for 1 hour. Then the nanoparticles deposited on basil seed mucilage were separated by a magnet and washed three times with deionized water and finally dried for 18 hours in an oven at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.6 Synthesis of magnetic ZIF-8 nanocomposites (NP/MC/ZIF-8)
A) First, \(2.5\,\mathrm{g}\) of zinc nitrate \(6\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added to \(75\,\mathrm{mL}\) methanol and stirred for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) by a magnetic stirrer. Then \(1\,\mathrm{g}\) of magnetic magnetite/basil mucilage nanocomposite was added to the initial solution for 1 hour and mixed by a magnetic stirrer. B) Then \(125\,\mathrm{mL}\) methanol was added to \(24.5\,\mathrm{g}\) of 2-methylimidazole and the solution was stirred for 15 minutes with a magnetic stirrer. The remaining solution in step (B) was added to the solution in step (A) and stirred for 20 hours by a magnetic stirrer. Then the precipitate formed by the magnet was separated and washed three times with methanol and finally dried in an oven at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 hours.
## 2.7 Characterization
X-ray Diffraction (XRD-PW1730) was used to identify the structural properties of particles. Investigation of composition surface characteristics was performed by Field Scanning
Electron Microscopy (FESEM-MIRA3). Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometer (FTIR-AVATAR) was used to identify the functional groups. Measurement of the specific surface area was performed by nitrogen adsorption-desorption BET analysis by the device of the BELSORP MINI II model. The magnetic properties were assessed using a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM-MPMS-5 SQUID). Spectrophotometer Model (RAYLEIGH UV-2601) was used to measure the concentration of dye solutions.
## 2.8 Adsorption experiments
Adsorption kinetics experiments were done to find the equilibrium time at concentrations of 6 and \(8~\mathrm{mg/L}\) g of \(0.1~\mathrm{g}\) of nano-adsorbent in \(200~\mathrm{ml}\) volume of MB and EBT solutions. After performing the above-mentioned steps on preparing the adsorbent and starting the adsorption process during the times 0,5,10,15,20,30,40,60,120,180,240 minutes, the containers containing the solution were sampled using a magnet. The absorbance of the samples was measured by spectrophotometer. It has to be noted that to find a more accurate time to reach equilibrium, initially smaller time intervals were selected and later larger ones. Finally, the adsorbent capacity used at each time was calculated as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}=\frac{(\mathbf{C}_{0}-\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}})\mathbf{V}}{\mathbf{m}}\end{array}
\]
(1)
In this equation, \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}\) is the adsorbent capacity at time t \((\mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{g})\) , \(\mathbf{C_{0}}\) the initial concentration \((\mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{L})\) , \(\mathbf{C_{t}}\) the concentration at time t \((\mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{L})\) , \(\mathrm{v}\) the volume of solution (L), and m the value of adsorbent (g)[50].
Adsorption isotherm experiments were done at temperatures of 30 and \(45\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and initial concentrations of 2, 4, 6, and 8 MB and EBT in a volume of \(20\ \mathrm{ml}\) and \(0.01\ \mathrm{g}\) of nano-adsorbent at \(\mathrm{pH}=6\) to examine the effect of temperature and thermodynamic study. After reaching equilibrium time (which was considered 2 hours in this study according to the
kinetic experiments), the magnet was sampled from the solutions and the absorbance of the samples was measured by a spectrophotometer. The data were described using different isotherm models. Finally, to examine the effect of pH on the adsorption rate, \(0.01\ \mathrm{g}\) of nanoadsorbent was added to \(20\ \mathrm{ml}\) of \(8\ \mathrm{mg/L}\) MB and EBT whose pH was at 2,4,6,8,10 and after 2 hours stirring the adsorption medium was stopped and the solutions were sampled using a magnet and their absorption was measured using a spectrophotometer.
Finally, the equilibrium adsorption capacity and adsorption efficiency were calculated using the equilibrium concentration calibration curve and the following equations.
\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathbf{q}_e=\frac{(\mathbf{C}_0-\mathbf{C}_e)\mathbf{V}}{\mathbf{m}}\end{array}
\]
(2)
In this equation, \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\) is the equilibrium capacity \((\mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{g})\) , \({\bf C}_{0}\) the initial concentration \((\mathrm{mg}\,/\,\mathrm{L})\) , \(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}\) the equilibrium concentration \((\mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{L})\) . V is the volume of solution (L), m is adsorbent value (g) [51].
\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathrm{R}=\frac{C_0-C_e}{C_0}\times100\%\end{array}
\]
(3)
In this equation, \(\mathbf{R}\) is the adsorption efficiency (\%), \(\mathbf{C}_{0}\) initial concentration \((\mathrm{mg/L})\), and \(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}\) equilibrium concentration \((\mathrm{mg/L})\) [52].
## 2.9 Regeneration Experiments
To perform the adsorption reduction test, MB and EBT solutions at initial concentrations of \(2{-}8\ \mathrm{mg}\ /\ \mathrm{L}\) were added \(0.01\ \mathrm{g}\) of the sorbent. After 2 hours, the magnets were sampled with solutions and their absorption was measured by spectrophotometer. The adsorbent was then washed in distilled water, industrial ethanol, and distilled water, respectively, for three hours, and again placed in the shaker for 2 hours. The cycle was repeated 4 times and the reusability of the adsorbent for adsorption processes was evaluated.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 FESEM results
Fig.s (2-a), (2-b) and (2-c) showed FESEM images for the ZIF-8, NP / MC and NP / MC / ZIF-8 images, respectively. According to Fig. (2-a), one can state that the ZIF-8 sample has a porous crystal structure and a uniform particle size distribution[53]. Moreover, one can state that according to an atomic array (single-cell shape), it has a hexagonal crystalline system. According to Fig.s (2-a) and (2-b) and the final shape of the nanocomposite (2-c), the morphology present in ZIF-8 can be seen in the final composite and, by maintaining structure, ZIF-8 is coated on the magnetic core. Moreover, by adding magnetite magnetic nanoparticles coated with basil seed mucilage, the average particle size has increased and is formed according to the existing records and compared with existing models [54].
## 3.2 XRD results
Fig. 3 indicates the XRD pattern of samples ZIF-8, NP, NP/MC, and NP/MC/ZIF-8. Characteristic peaks of the ZIF-8 sample were observed at \(2\theta = 7.35\), 10.4, 12.75, 18.1, 26.75, and 29.75. The magnetite crystals had characteristic peaks in \(2\theta = 30.37\), 35.81, 57.41, and 63.17 [55]. As shown in Fig. 3, by comparison with the standard component characteristic peaks, no significant change in the location of the two-component NP and ZIF-8 peaks occurred during the synthesis of NP / MC nanoparticles as well as the magnetic nanofabricated ZIF-8 / MC / NP synthesis. Is. These results indicate the formation of ZIF-8 / MC / NP magnetic nanocomposite.
## 3.3 FTIR results
Fig. 4 shows the infrared spectrum of samples of ZIF-8, NP, MC, NP / MC, and NP / MC / ZIF-8. In ZIF-8 infrared spectroscopy analysis, the spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(422.3551\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belongs to the Zn-N bond. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of 43.754 and \(689.12~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is related to the extracellular binding of the imidazole ring. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(3448.29~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belongs to the tensile vibration of N-H in the imidazole organic ligand. The spectra at the absorption frequency of 1580 and \(1305.85~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) relate to \(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{N}\) bond and C-N bond, respectively. The spectra at the absorption frequency 2926.46 and \(3131.05~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are related to the tensile vibration of the symmetric bonds \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) and the tensile vibration of the asymmetric bonds \(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}\), respectively [56, 57]. In the spectroscopic analysis of magnetite infrared spectroscopy, the spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(559.3379~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is related to the Fe-O bond. The spectra in the absorption frequency 3423.534 and \(1629.795~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are respectively related to the hydrogen bond created by the OH group (tensile vibration) and the bending vibrations of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules[58]. In the infrared spectroscopic analysis of mucilage obtained from basil seed, the spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(900{-}1050~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be related to C-O and C-O-H bonds. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(1269.37\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is related to the C-O bond. The spectrum at the absorption frequency \(2925.06~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) shows the bond of \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) methyl groups. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of \(3428.39~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) shows the hydroxyl alcohol groups. The spectra at the absorption frequencies 1615.97 and \(1417.53~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belong to the asymmetric tensile bond - COO and the symmetric tensile bond - COO, respectively. The spectrum at the absorption frequency \(1047.19~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is related to the C-O-C stretch. The -COO group shows the presence of uric acid in basil seed mucilage [59].
The observed spectra of NP/MC nanocomposite at the absorption frequencies of 586.5, 1631.25, and \(3431.70~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) related to magnetite magnetic nanoparticles and spectra in the adsorption frequencies are 900-1050, 2925.36, 2925.36-3431.70, and \(1631.25~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\),
respectively, related to basil seed mucilage. The observed spectra of NP / MC / ZIF-8 magnetic nanocomposite at absorption frequencies 478.2834, 678.86, 1303.11, 1593.99, 2920.32, and \(3179.88~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were related to ZIF-8, spectra at absorption frequencies of 571.10, 3446.44, and \(1593.99~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for magnetite magnetic nanoparticles and spectra at frequencies adsorption of basil seed mucilage is about 900-1050, 1433.59, and \(2851.34~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were related to mucilage from basil seeds. Thus, one can state that NP / MC / ZIF-8 magnetic nanocomposite and NP/MC Nano-composite peaks of both nanocomposites did not change significantly, indicating that the nanocomposite components without certain chemical reactions are interconnected and combined.
## 3.4 BET results
The results of this BET analysis show that ZIF-8 has a specific surface area of \(1436.2\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{gr}^{-1}\), a total pore volume of \(329.98~3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and an average pore diameter is \(3.184~\mathrm{nm}\), which is significant and very appropriate compared to the values in the background of the study [60]. According to the BJH diagram, one can state that ZIF-8 cavities synthesized are mesocavities. Fig. (5-b) shows that the adsorption and desorption of ZIF-8 follow the type 4 isotherm. The results showed that the specific surface area of ZIF-8 / MC / NP magnetic nanocomposite was \(7.109~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{gr}^{-1}\). The decrease in specific surface area in the final nanocomposite indicates that some of the ZIF-8 / NP magnetic nanocomposites are occupied by basil seed mucilage. Moreover, in the final composite the cross-section of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) is about \(14\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{gr}^{-1}\) and since the predominant weight of the composite is \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), it decreases the amount of BET in the final composite. Furthermore, for NP/MC/ZIF-8 magnetic composite the total volume of cavities was \(1.6108\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the mean cavity diameter was 13.763 nm. Additionally, based on BJH charts, one can state that synthesized nanocomposites are
meso-cavity type. Fig. (5-e) shows that the adsorption and desorption of NP/MC/ZIF-8 follows isotherms of type 2 and 4.
## 3.5 VSM results
One of the properties of the synthesized nanocomposite in this study is the magnetic property for facilitating the separation of the adsorbent and adsorbent solutions, determined by the VSM method. As the intensity of the external magnetic field increases, the magnetization of the environment increases rapidly, and as the magnitude of the applied field decreases as the magnetization increases, and this decreasing trend continues until saturation magnetization reaches its saturation value. As the field intensity decreases more, the magnetic induction decreases and eventually reaches its negative saturation. As Fig. 6 shows, one can state that S-shaped NP, NP / MC, and NP / MC / ZIF-3 diagrams have superparamagnetic properties that can be removed by the magnet from the solution after the adsorption process. The saturation magnetization for NP, NP / MC, and NP / MC / ZIF-8 magnetic nanocomposites is about 74.03, 13.45 and 2.22 emu·g·t\(^{-1}\). According to the results obtained, one can state that magnetization to magnetite nanoparticles and ZIF-8 to magnetite nanoparticles with mucilage coating extracted from host seed decreased the magnetic properties, which was for two reasons: 1) because of the coatings by mucilage and ZIF-8 on the magnetite nanoparticles and 2) magnetic property per unit mass is expressed as the denominator of this fraction once because of the mucilage coating on the magnetite nanoparticles and again because of the ZIF-8 coating increased with the addition of ZIF-8 this increased the denominator of the fraction and decreased the maximum magnetic property. However, the final adsorbent still has a superparamagnetic property that could be removed from the solution by the magnet after the adsorption process.
## 3.6 Adsorption kinetics
3.6 Adsorption kineticsOne of the significant studies in the adsorption process is to examine the effect of soluble contact time on the adsorbent. Moreover, it is possible to predict the adsorption mechanism and the rate-limiting steps of absorption like mass transfer or chemical reaction, by examining the discontinuous adsorption kinetics. Fig. 7 and 8 indicate the changes in MB and EBT dye concentration in the solution over time as well as the mass changes of both dyes adsorbed for the adsorbent mass (absorption capacity changes of both dyes). According to Fig.s (7-a) and (8-a), the concentration decreases with time, and the absorption rate decreases as the slope of the graph decrease, and after 2 hours the concentration of the dyes in the solution is almost constant, called the equilibrium concentration \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\). Moreover, as shown in Fig.s (7-b) and (8-b), the adsorption capacity increases and the rate of absorption decreased as the rate of absorption decreased. The equilibrium time of adsorption by the adsorbent reached its maximum value, which is called the equilibrium concentration capacity \(\left(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\), occurring after 2 hours of relative equilibrium here. Moreover, we obtain the experimental results obtained by the model to examine the adsorption kinetics process. We examined the experimental results obtained by pseudo-first-order, pseudo-second-order, intra-molecular, Boyd, and Elovich models to examine the process of adsorption kinetics.
The linear form of the pseudo-first-order model is expressed as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\log(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}})=\log\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\frac{\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{e}}}{2.303}\mathbf{t}\end{array}
\]
(4)
The linear form of the pseudo-second-order model is expressed as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\frac{t}{q_t}=\frac{1}{q_e^2k_2t}+\frac{1}{q_e}t\end{array}
\]
(5)
Elovich model is expressed as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}=\frac{1}{\upbeta}\ln(\upalpha\upbeta)+\frac{1}{\upbeta}\ln(\mathrm{t})\end{array}
\]
(6)
Boyd model is expressed as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{r}F=1-\frac{6}{\pi^2}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}\exp(-n^2B_t)\end{array}
\]
(7)
Where \( F \) is the attainment of any equilibrium at various times \( t \), and \( B_{t} \) is a function of \( F \):
\[
\begin{array}{r}F=\frac{q_t}{q_e}\end{array}
\]
(8)
Equation (7) shows that is not possible to calculate \( B_{t} \) values for each absorbed fraction. Using the Fourier transforms and then the merger, Reichenberg obtained the following approximations:
For values of \( F > 0.85 \):
\[
Bt=-0.4977-\ln(1-F)
\]
(9)
For values of \( F < 0.85 \):
\[
\begin{array}{r}Bt=(\sqrt{\pi}-\sqrt{\pi-\left(\frac{\pi^2F}{3}\right)}).\end{array}
\]
(10)
The intra-molecular infiltration model is expressed as follows:
\[
q_{t}=k_{i}t^{0/5}+I
\]
(11)
In the above equations, \( q_{t} \) is the adsorbent capacity at time \( t \) (mg / g), \( q_{t} \) the equilibrium capacity (mg / g), \( t \) time, and \( k_{1} \) pseudo-first-order rate constant \( (min^{-1}) \), \( k_{2} \) pseudo-model constant second-order \( (gmg^{-1}min^{-1}) \), the parameters \( \alpha \) and \( \beta \) are constants of the Elovich model, where \( \alpha \) is the initial absorption rate of the equation \( (gmg^{-1}min^{-1}) \) and \( \beta \) is the
repulsion constant \((\mathrm{gmg}^{-1})\), \(k_{i}\) is the intra-particle infiltration rate constant \((mg g^{-1} t^{-0.5})\) and I am the intra-particle infiltration constant \((mg g^{-1})\). The pseudo-first-order model introduces the adsorption process as physical adsorption. This shows that this type of mechanism is the rate-limiting step of the diffusion and does not depend on the concentration of both reactants, that is, physical exchange[61]. The pseudo-second-order model helps determine the chemical nature of a particular adsorption process. In this model, the velocity control step is a chemical reaction, and the pseudo-second-order chemical reaction kinetics brings about the best correlation of the experimental data and the control of the adsorption mechanism by the chemical velocity. This is why it is known as a chemical absorber[62]. Elovich equation is one of the most common equations to describe the kinetics of inhomogeneous chemical adsorption of gases on solid surfaces[63]. This model is favorable in chemical adsorption processes and is suitable for systems with heterogeneous adsorption surfaces[64]. Boyd model determines whether the main mass transfer resistance in the thin film (boundary layer) is around the adsorbent particles or is the diffusion resistance within the pores. By drawing the Bt diagram over time, if the diagram is linear and crosses the source, then the rate of mass transfer is controlled by pore penetration. If the graph is nonlinear or linear but does not cross the origin, the film penetration or chemical reaction controls the absorption rate[65]. The intra-particle infiltration can be described in three successive stages: 1) Transfer of adsorbed material from the soluble mass to the outer surface of the adsorbent by molecular diffusion 2) Internal diffusion, transfer of adsorbed material from the particle surface to internal sites, and 3) adsorption of soluble particles (adsorbed material particles) from the active sites to the inner surface of the cavities. After plotting qt in terms of \(t^{0.5}\) if the intra-particle diffusion constant was zero, the whole process can be controlled by diffusivity in porosity; otherwise, the graph is composed of several parts[66]. Parameter \(R^{2}\) shows the deviation of the laboratory data from the desired model, showing that the proximity of this
parameter to one shows less deviation than the target model. According to Table (1), by comparing the values of \( \mathbf{R}^{2} \), one can state that MB is consistent with all models but has more consistency with the Elovich model and the pseudo-second-order model for both concentrations 6 and 8 (mg/L). One can state that the adsorption surfaces are heterogeneous and the speed control step is a chemical reaction. Additionally, according to the Boyd model, one can state that as the diagram is linear but did not cross the origin of the penetration in the porosity, it does not determine the rate of adsorption process and the influence on the film or chemical reaction controls the rate of adsorption process and according to the diffusion model. The intra-molecular process is performed in three steps: adsorption on the outer surface (penetration in the film), adsorption on the inner surface (penetration on the porosity) and equilibrium, and the results of the intra-particle diffusion model confirm Boyd model results. According to Table (1), by comparing the values of \( \mathbf{R}^{2} \) obtained in EBT dye, it can be said to fit all models but is more consistent with the pseudo-second-order model for both 6 and 8 (mg / L) concentrations. This means that the speed control step is a chemical reaction. Moreover, according to Boyd model, one can state that as the diagram is linear but did not cross the origin of the penetration in the porosity, it does not determine the rate of adsorption process and the influence on the film or chemical reaction controls the rate of adsorption process and according to the diffusion model. The intra-molecular process is performed in three steps: adsorption on the outer surface (penetration in the film), adsorption on the inner surface (penetration on the porosity) and equilibrium, and the results of the intra-particle diffusion model confirm Boyd model results.
## 3.7 Adsorption isotherm
The adsorption isotherm diagrams (Fig.s (9-a) and (10-a)) show the equilibrium relationship between equilibrium adsorption capacity \( \left(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\right) \) and the equilibrium concentration of the
adsorbed material at the specified temperature, using the adaptation of the experimental data to Freundlich and Langmuir models to describe this relationship. The linear form of the Freundlich isotherm can be expressed by the following equation:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\log(q_e)=\log(K_F)+\frac{1}{n}\log(C_e)\end{array}
\]
The linear form of the Langmuir equation can be expressed by the following equation:
\[
\begin{array}{r}\frac{C_e}{\mathfrak{q}_\mathrm{e}}=\frac{1}{q_mk_l}+\frac{1}{q_m}C_e\end{array}
\]
(13)
The appropriate nature of the adsorption and the specific properties of the Langmuir adsorption companion can be described by a non-unit constant called the separation factor or equilibrium parameter introduced by Hall et al. in 1966:
\[
\begin{array}{r}R_{l}=\frac{1}{1+k_{l}C_{0}}\end{array}
\]
(14)
In Equation (13), if \(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{l}}\) equals zero, it shows an irreversible absorption, \(0<\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{l}}<1\) that represents desirable adsorption, \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{l}}=1\) denotes linear adsorption and \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{l}}>1\) denotes undesirable adsorption.
In the above equations, \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\) is equilibrium capacity \((\mathrm{mg}\,/\,\mathrm{g})\) , \(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}\) equilibrium concentration (mg / L), \(\mathrm{K_{F}}\) Freundlich  adsorption  capacity \((\mathrm{L}g^{-1})\) , \(\frac{1}{n}\) heterogeneity  factor  and  denotes adsorption intensity, \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{m}}\) maximum adsorption by the adsorbent in Langmuir isotherm model \((\mathrm{mg}\,/\,\mathrm{g})\) , \(\mathrm{k_{l}}\) Langmuir constant \((\mathrm{L}\,/\,\mathrm{mg})\) , and \(\mathbf{C}_{0}\) is the highest initial adsorbed concentrations \((\mathrm{mg}\,/\,\mathrm{L})\) .
Freundlich model is an experimental multilayer adsorption isotherm for non-ideal adsorption onto heterogeneous surfaces[67]. Freundlich parameters, \(\mathrm{K_{F}}\), and \(n\) are, respectively, related to the adsorption capacity and absorption intensity. Moreover, one has to note that the parameter \(\frac{1}{n}\) must be between 0 and 1. As \(\frac{1}{n}\) value approaches 0, the adsorbent surface
becomes more heterogeneous[68]. Langmuir isotherm model represents the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent that predicts single-layer adsorption on a homogeneous surface without any interaction between the adsorbed molecules. This model assumes continuous adsorption energy on the surface and does not predict any interaction of adsorbed materials on the surface[61].
According to Fig.s (9) and (10) and by comparing \( \mathbf{R}^{2} \) values obtained, one can state that both MB and EBT are compatible with both Freundlich and Langmuir models but are more consistent with the Freundlich model. This means that the adsorbent surface is heterogeneous, there is the interaction between the adsorbed particles and the adsorption is multilayer. Additionally, the values of \( \frac{1}{n} \) calculated by the Freundlich model were less than 1, showing optimum adsorption conditions.
