## 3.9 The effect of pH on adsorption properties
According to Fig. (11-a), MB removal behavior with the change in \(\mathrm{pH}\) indicates a higher percentage of removal under basic conditions than in acidic conditions. MB is a cationic dye thus producing color cations due to dissolution in water. As \(\mathrm{pH}\) value decreases, \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)ions increase in the environment. Thus, the adsorbent surface becomes protonated and the negative sites on the adsorbent surface become more protruding, and electrostatic repulsion is created between MB and the adsorbent surface. The increase in \(\mathrm{pH}\) increases \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)ion in the solution, following which negative active sites are created on the adsorbent surface. Thus, an electrostatic interaction occurs between MB and the adsorbent surface after which adsorption increases[73].
EBT is an anionic dye due to its sulfonate (-SO3) groups having a negative charge in aqueous solution. At low \(\mathrm{pH}\), the adsorbent surface charge density increased because of the increase in \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)ions, and consequently, the adsorption of EBT increased by electrostatic adsorption
between the dye and the adsorbent. Hence, according to Fig. (13-b), the highest adsorption occurred at \(\mathrm{pH}=2\) and with the increase in \(\mathrm{pH}\) to \(\mathrm{pH}=5\) due to electrostatic repulsion between the dye and the adsorbent resulting from the formation of negative active sites on the adsorbent surface, EBT adsorption decreases [74, 75]. At \(\mathrm{pH}=6-10\), the color of the EBT solution changed from purple to brown to dark blue, which shows instability in EBT structure at \(\mathrm{pH}\) above 5. Therefore, Fig. (13-b) shows a change in the diagram process at \(\mathrm{pH}=6-10\) and due to the degradation of EBT structure, this \(\mathrm{pH}\) range the observed adsorption capacity does not mean an increase in adsorption but a decrease in the concentration resulting from degradation [76]. Different literatures have reported dye adsorption processes, such as electrostatic interactions, hydrophobic interactions, \(\pi-\pi\) stacking interactions, and hydrogen bonding interactions. The EBT is a negatively charged anionic dye, and ZIF-8 is positively charged under acidic conditions. Strong electrostatic interactions can be easily generated, thereby excellent adsorption properties could be exhibited [77]. Based on the \(\mathrm{pH}\) effect analysis, the electrostatic interaction plays the dominant role for the adsorption of EBT under acidic conditions, while hydrogen bonding and \(\pi-\pi\) interaction existed in all conditions. For the adsorption of MB, electrostatic interaction, hydrogen bonding, and \(\pi-\pi\) interaction are the main adsorption mechanisms under alkaline conditions. In the acidic or neutral condition, hydrogen bonding and \(\pi-\pi\) interaction played a significant role in the adsorption of MB [78]. On the other hand, it has been reported that ionic bonding between the negatively charged functional groups of MB \(\left(-\mathrm{SO}^{3-}\right)\) and positively charged center of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})^{+}\right)\)can be formed [79].
## 3.10 Cycle regeneration
In the recovery of magnetic ZIF-8 nanocomposite by magnetite nanocomposite with mucilage coating extracted from basil seed as an adsorbent in MB adsorption process,
adsorbent recovery at each step increased adsorbent capacity in MB removal. This behavior can show that industrial ethanol is an exceptional detergent for adsorbent recovery. Besides, one can state that the MB adsorption process by magnetic ZIF-8 nanocomposite by magnetite nanocomposite with mucilage coating extracted from basil seed is a reversible chemical process. Indeed, we can state that MB is a light dye and ethanol is an amphoteric acid acting here as an electrostatic attraction, increasing the adsorption capacity [63]. In magnetic ZIF-8 nanocomposite recovery by magnetite nanocomposite with mucilage coating extracted from basil seed as an adsorbent in the EBT adsorption process, the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent decreased at each step, which could be due to the occupation of potential sites by colored molecules. In adsorbent recovery to remove both MB and EBT, the results indicate that the adsorbent can still remove dye so it can be a usable adsorbent for the removal of MB and EBT (Fig. 12). Moreover, the composite material exhibited high structural stability even after multi-cycles use; such novel material is particularly applicable for removal of MB and EBT dyes from environmental samples. Therefore, the fabricated composite material can be used as potential material for the detection and removal of dyes from waste solutions as an effective material in large-scale use.
## 4. Conclusion
NP / MC / ZIF-8 magnetic nanocomposite was successfully synthesized as an adsorbent and used to absorb MB and EBT. In the dye adsorption process by the adsorbent, the experimental data are consistent with the kinetic models showing that the adsorption surfaces are heterogeneous, the speed control step is done by chemical reaction, and the process is performed in three external adsorption steps. Moreover, according to the match between in vitro data with adsorption isotherm models for both MB and EBT, one can state that the
adsorbent surface is heterogeneous, there is the interaction between the adsorbed particles, and adsorption is multilayer. Thermodynamic studies indicate that the adsorption process of both MB and EBT is an endothermic reaction that is associated with an increase in irregularity. In the process of MB adsorption, positive \(\Delta\mathbf{G}\) values at all temperatures show a Non-spontaneous process and in the process of EBT adsorption, negative \(\Delta\mathbf{G}\) values at all temperatures show a spontaneous process. The results of adsorption experiments in \(\mathrm{pH}=2-10\) range for MB showed a higher percentage of removal under basic conditions compared to acidic conditions and in the case of EBT showed a higher percentage of removal under acidic conditions compared to basic conditions. At \(\mathrm{pH}=6-10\), given the instability of the dye structure at \(\mathrm{pH}\) above 5, the observed increase in adsorption capacity does not mean an increase in adsorption yet a decrease in concentration resulting from dye degradation. Moreover, the composite material exhibited high structural stability even after multi-cycles use; such novel material is particularly applicable for removal of MB and EBT dyes from environmental samples. Therefore, the fabricated composite material can be used as potential material for the detection and removal of dyes from waste solutions as an effective material in large-scale use. In summary, the synthesized nanocomposite has the potential to be applied in different water treatment applications for the removal of dyes. The data suggested that the provided results are encouraging for the real application in large-scale for the environmental dye-containing water samples. Therefore, the removal of dyes by the sustainable nanocomposite material is an alternative to better use of wastewater treatment, being economically beneficial, and collaborating with the environment.
